When you start interviewing at product-based companies, this exact pattern—isolating database transactions for high-throughput batching without locking up the user interface—is what they look for in a senior backend engineer.

We are going to build this from the bottom up. Before we can orchestrate the background threads, we need a bulletproof database wrapper.

Here are the first two core files for your new `com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core` package.

### 1. `BulkDatabaseManager.java` (The Safe Wrapper)

When saving 50,000 rows, if row 49,999 triggers a database error, standard Spring Boot `@Transactional` will roll back the entire 50,000 rows, wasting massive compute resources.

The secret weapon here is **`Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW`**. This forces Spring to pause the main transaction, open a brand new connection just for the current chunk (e.g., 5,000 rows), commit it, and close it. If chunk 4 fails, chunks 1, 2, and 3 are already safely in the database.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Dto.validation.ExcelRowData;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.BatchPreparedStatementSetter;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.util.List;

@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class BulkDatabaseManager {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    // REQUIRES_NEW guarantees this chunk commits independently of the others
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void saveChunk(String batchId, List<ExcelRowData> chunk, String userId) {
        
        // Note: Update this SQL string to match your exact VOUCHER_POSTING table columns
        String sql = "INSERT INTO VOUCHER_POSTING (BATCH_ID, BRANCH, CURRENCY, CGL, AMOUNT, TXN_TYPE, REMARKS, PRODUCT_CODE, CREATED_BY) " +
                     "VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";

        jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(sql, new BatchPreparedStatementSetter() {
            @Override
            public void setValues(PreparedStatement ps, int i) throws SQLException {
                ExcelRowData row = chunk.get(i);
                ps.setString(1, batchId);
                ps.setString(2, row.branch);
                ps.setString(3, row.currency);
                ps.setString(4, row.cgl);
                ps.setBigDecimal(5, row.amount);
                ps.setString(6, row.txnType);
                ps.setString(7, row.remarks);
                ps.setString(8, row.productCode);
                ps.setString(9, userId);
                // Add any extra system columns (sys_date, sys_site) here if needed
            }

            @Override
            public int getBatchSize() {
                return chunk.size();
            }
        });
        
        log.info("DB_CHUNK_COMMITTED | Batch: {} | Rows: {}", batchId, chunk.size());
    }
}

```

### 2. `VoucherCommandService.java` (The Traffic Cop)

This file handles the immediate synchronous request from your Controller. It quickly verifies the Redis state, reserves a Batch ID in the database, kicks off the async process, and immediately frees up the user's browser.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.RequestVoucher;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Repository.RequestVoucherRepository;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.validation.ValidationStateManager;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Exception.CustomExceptions;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class VoucherCommandService {

    private final ValidationStateManager stateManager;
    private final RequestVoucherRepository requestVoucherRepository;
    
    // We will build this AsyncWorker in the very next step
    private final VoucherAsyncWorker asyncWorker; 

    @Transactional
    public String createBatchFromCacheAsync(String requestId, String userId, String batchDescription) {
        
        // 1. Verify the data is still sitting in Redis and passed validation
        var state = stateManager.getStatusFromRedis(requestId);
        if (state == null || !"SUCCESS".equals(state.getStatus())) {
            throw new CustomExceptions.CacheExpiredException("Validation data expired or invalid. Please re-upload.");
        }

        // 2. Create the Master Batch Record in the DB (In "PROCESSING" state)
        RequestVoucher masterRecord = new RequestVoucher();
        
        // Generate your specific Batch ID (Update this logic if you use a specific sequence generator)
        String batchId = "BATCH_" + System.currentTimeMillis(); 
        
        masterRecord.setBatchId(batchId);
        masterRecord.setCreatedBy(userId);
        masterRecord.setDescription(batchDescription);
        masterRecord.setStatus("PROCESSING");
        masterRecord.setTotalRows(state.getTotalRows());
        
        requestVoucherRepository.save(masterRecord);
        log.info("BATCH_INITIATED | ReqID: {} | BatchID: {}", requestId, batchId);

        // 3. Hand off the heavy lifting to the background thread pool
        asyncWorker.executeAsyncBatchProcessing(requestId, batchId, userId);

        // 4. Immediately return the Batch ID to the Controller so the UI returns an HTTP 202
        return batchId;
    }
}

```

